WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
Okay, we have another simple concept question being asked today.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.279
So which of the following statements about factors of safety is true about soils near

00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:13.839
occupied structures?

00:00:13.839 --> 00:00:14.839
1.

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:20.120
The factor of safety against soil heave is less than the factor of safety against rotational

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.000
failure for a stable slope.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.000
2.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.879
The factor of safety against soil heave is greater than the factor of safety against

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:32.119
for a stable slope.

00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:33.039
3.

00:00:33.039 --> 00:00:38.100
The factor of safety against soil heave is the same as the factor of safety against rotational

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.039
failure for a stable slope.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:42.079
4.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:47.280
The factor of safety against soil heave and the factor of safety against rotational

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:52.739
failure for a stable slope will depend upon their own calculations for each case.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:58.740
And what we can see here is that number two is going to be the correct answer.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:05.740
Typically, soil heave receives a factor of safety of somewhere between 4 and 5,

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:12.740
whereas rotational failure for a stable soil will have a factor of safety closer to 1.5.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:21.740
And usually, the soil heave is higher due to the fact that the heave analysis contains a lot of inherent inaccuracies in the calculations

00:01:21.739 --> 00:01:24.739
that need to be accounted for.

00:01:24.739 --> 00:01:29.739
So the factor of safety is more conservative there to guard against any unknowns.

00:01:29.739 --> 00:01:32.739
And that's really it for this problem.

00:01:32.739 --> 00:01:37.739
Not all concept questions are able to be searched for in the reference manual,

00:01:37.739 --> 00:01:44.739
but if you can find those equations, you might be able to find something about the factors of safety.

00:01:44.739 --> 00:01:49.739
Otherwise, just do your best with these and don't beat yourself up if you don't know them.
